# The string literals that are not messages — spec 15, D3 and D10, issue #661.
#
# `src/scripts/check-translatable-strings.py` counts every string literal of `src/lib` and
# `src/exe` that has a word in it and does not pass through the translation API. This table is
# the one place that says which of them are not for a reader, and why: what it matches is never
# counted. Everything else is, until it is wrapped in `translate`, `translateIn`,
# `translatePlural` or `tr`.
#
# A section opens with `[category]` and the reason that category is never translated. Each
# entry below it is one of:
#
#   text      <regex>   the literal's whole text matches (Python `re.fullmatch`)
#   call      <name>    the call that directly receives the literal has this name
#   statement <regex>   the statement holding the literal matches; the literal is written `@`
#   file      <glob> [<regex>]
#                       a literal of a matching file — every one, or those whose whole text
#                       matches the regex: a vocabulary that is data in that file only
#
# An entry is added with the reason that justifies it, and reviewed like code: an exemption
# that is too wide hides the very messages the ratchet is there to find.

[identifier] A name the program matches or writes — a settings key, a diagnostic code, a JSON key, an object or icon name. It is an identifier, not a word, and translating it would break whatever reads it.
text      [a-z][a-z0-9]*(?:[._-][a-z0-9]+)+[._-]?
text      [a-z][a-z0-9]*[._-]
text      [a-z]+(?:[A-Z][a-z0-9]*)+
call      setObjectName
# ISO 15924's code of the script common to every language, by which the correction patterns
# are filed (#665).
text      Zyyy
# The language the spelling falls back to when the system names none: a code Enchant reads (#665).
file      src/lib/subedit/gui/join_split_page.cpp en
# The code of the source language, which Preferences offers whatever is installed (#669).
file      src/lib/subedit/gui/preferences_dialog.cpp en
# The code of the source language, first of the installed languages every surface lists (#716).
file      src/lib/subedit/core/libraries.cpp en
# The key CLI11's formatter looks a word of its help up by; what it shows is translated (#663).
call      label
# The values of `--format json` the command line writes, one word each: a kind of failure, of
# line ending, of document, a verdict, an origin, a method, a surface (#663).
file      src/lib/subedit/cli/records.* [a-z]+
file      src/lib/subedit/cli/inspection.cpp lf|crlf|cr|clean|partial|silent|asked|detected
file      src/lib/subedit/cli/pairing.cpp position|number
file      src/lib/subedit/cli/changes.cpp main|translation
file      src/lib/subedit/cli/configuring.cpp both

[json] The `--format json` output is a contract: its keys and values are the same in every language (D3).
statement \.set\(
statement ^@,\s*(?:count\(|static_cast<std::int64_t>\(|Json\b|std::vector<Count>|constraintsRecord\(|settings(?:File)?$|0$)
statement \b(?:counts|fields)\.emplace_back\(\s*@
file      src/lib/subedit/cli/json.cpp \S+

[option] Command-line option, sub-command and positional names, and the metavariables and values of an option. The user types them; they are never translated (D3).
text      --?[A-Za-z][\w-]*(?:\s*[,/]\s*--?[A-Za-z][\w-]*)*
text      [A-Za-z][\w,-]*(?:\|[A-Za-z][\w,-]*)+
statement \badd_(?:option|flag|subcommand)\(\s*@
statement \.name\s*=\s*@
call      option_text
call      default_str
call      IsMember
# The value that switches a limit of `adjust` off, as the user types it (#663).
file      src/lib/subedit/cli/adjusting.cpp off

[format] Names of subtitle formats and their option names, and names of character encodings (D3). A format is called by its name in every language.
text      SubRip|WebVTT|SubViewer 2|Sub Station Alpha|Advanced SSA|MicroDVD|MPL2|TMPlayer|LRC
text      srt|vtt|subviewer2|ssa|ass|microdvd|mpl2|tmplayer|lrc|sub|txt|json|text
text      (?:UTF|ISO|KOI8|EUC|CP|IBM)-?[\w-]*(?:,version=\d)?|windows-\d+|Shift_JIS|Big5|GB\d+|Unicode

[constant] An all-capitals token: an environment variable, a keyword of a file format, a placeholder of the syntax. It is a name, not a sentence.
text      [A-Z][A-Z0-9_]*(?:[ -][A-Z0-9_]+)*

[program] The names of the program, its executables and the programs it drives: a name is not translated.
text      subedit(?:-cli|-gui)?|libmpv|mpv|ffprobe|ffmpeg
# The window's title when nothing is open: the name, and `[*]`, the token where Qt puts the mark
# of unsaved changes (#664).
text      subedit\[\*\]
# The name before a version number: the line `--version` writes and the first line of About,
# « subedit 0.15.18 » in every language (#665).
text      subedit(?: %1| )

[path] A file name, an extension or a path.
text      \.?[\w.-]*\.[a-z0-9]{2,4}
text      \.[\w-]+
text      /[\w/.-]*
statement /\s*@|@\s*/

[bytes] Escape sequences and digit alphabets: bytes and characters the program reads or writes, not text.
text      (?:\\x[0-9A-Fa-f]{2})+
text      0123456789abcdef|abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz

[matched] A string compared with, or searched for in, data the program reads — a value of the settings file, a field of a pattern file, a flag of a regular expression. It matches what is written there, which is not in the user's language.
statement (?:==|!=)\s*(?:[\w:]+\s*[({]\s*)?@|@\s*[)}]?\s*(?:==|!=)
statement \.(?:find|rfind|starts_with|ends_with|startsWith|endsWith|contains|indexOf)\(\s*@
statement \b(?:get|need|needText|refuseValue|numberOf|attributeOf)\(\s*@

[settings-file] The example values written into the settings file: data, read back by the program.
call      writeOption

[player] The property names, commands and values of libmpv, the video player: its own vocabulary.
file      src/lib/subedit/gui/mpv_player.cpp [a-z][\w+-]*
call      mpv_set_property
call      mpv_set_property_string
call      mpv_get_property
call      mpv_command

[markup] Subtitle markup and escaping: tags, entities and override blocks of the formats, written into files.
file      src/lib/subedit/core/text/*markup* \S+
text      </?[A-Za-z][^>]*>?.*
text      &[a-z]+;
text      .*\{\\[^}]*\}?.*

[regex] A regular expression: what the program looks for, written in the syntax of the engine.
text      .*(?:\\[dDsSwWbBpPAzZ]|\(\?[:=!<]|\[\^|\\[1-9]).*

[log] Lines for developers, written to a log and not to the user, stay English (D3).
statement \bq(?:Debug|Info|Warning|Critical|Fatal)\(
statement \bstd::clog\b

[css] Qt style sheets: CSS, read by Qt and never shown.
call      setStyleSheet

[shortcut] Key sequences stay the Qt sequences in every language (D3).
call      setShortcut
text      (?:(?:Ctrl|Alt|Shift|Meta)\+)+\S+|F\d{1,2}

[file-syntax] What a subtitle file or a message catalogue contains: section headers, keywords and fields, read and written as the format defines them.
file      src/lib/subedit/core/format/*_syntax.hpp
file      src/lib/subedit/core/format/* \[[^\]]+\].*|[A-Z]\w*[:=] ?|\{[A-Z]+\}
file      src/lib/subedit/core/i18n/catalogue.cpp [\w-]+[:=]

[external-program] The arguments given to another program, in its own syntax.
file      src/lib/subedit/core/video/declared_frame_rate.cpp \S+

[language-name] A language's name in that language, as a list of languages shows it: a reader looks for the name of the language they read, written as they write it (#669).
file      src/lib/subedit/core/i18n/language_names.cpp

[spelling-data] What the spell checker matches in the subtitles' own text: English ordinal suffixes and contractions, data of the language checked and not of the interface.
file      src/lib/subedit/core/text/spell_checker.cpp \S+

[contract] The message of an exception thrown when a caller broke a precondition: a defect of the program, read by a developer in a crash report, never by the user (#684).
text      splitProject: nothing would stay, or nothing would leave
file      src/lib/subedit/core/model/project.cpp subtitle index | in a project of |restoring | subtitles at | destinations

[catalogue-loading] Why a message catalogue was refused. The catalogue is the very thing that failed to load, so it cannot say it in the user's language; the reason is read by the translator who wrote the file, in a test or a debugger, and `installedLanguage` drops it (#684).
file      src/lib/subedit/core/i18n/catalogue.cpp
file      src/lib/subedit/core/i18n/plural_expression.cpp

[setting-value] The words a value of the settings file is written with, and the sections that file's comments name: the user types or reads them as written, in every language (#684).
file      src/lib/subedit/core/config/settings.cpp em|characters|selection|main|translation|system|light|dark|above|below|number|start|end|duration|true|false
file      src/lib/subedit/core/config/settings.cpp #.*

[format-field] A name in a subtitle format or a pattern file: the style every Sub Station Alpha file declares, the fields and attributes a pattern file is read by, the file extension of a pattern kind, the marker a format writes for a line break (#684).
text      Default
text      \[br\]
file      src/lib/subedit/core/text/correction_pattern.cpp capitalization
file      src/lib/subedit/core/text/pattern_catalogue.cpp Group|Penalty|name|enabled|true

[tool-output] What an external tool writes and the program reads, and the values it is given: the keys of whisper.cpp's JSON, the marker of its progress lines, the magic number of a model, the tags of `ffprobe`, the keywords of JSON itself. The tool's syntax, not words for a reader (#774, #775, #776).
file      src/lib/subedit/core/transcription/audio_source.cpp \S+
file      src/lib/subedit/core/transcription/whisper_cpp_output.cpp progress =|\S+
file      src/lib/subedit/core/transcription/whisper_cpp_engine.cpp en|auto
file      src/lib/subedit/core/io/json_reader.cpp true|false|null|bfnrt
